WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.088
The slides describe an early RAMEN implementation
using sharded connections and Redis, followed by a

00:00:06.088 --> 00:00:10.472
redesign separating connection handling, routing and
cluster management.

00:00:10.472 --> 00:00:15.282
Treat the throughput and connection counts as
reported historical observations.

00:00:15.282 --> 00:00:21.248
They illustrate why topology convergence and
overloaded event loops can become important even
when

00:00:21.248 --> 00:00:26.971
each individual message is small and the
application’s business logic appears
straightforward.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:33.388
The case identifies long-tail delivery problems
involving acknowledgement loss, inconsistent client

00:00:33.388 --> 00:00:37.389
behavior and limitations of the earlier
unidirectional stream.

00:00:37.389 --> 00:00:43.713
Its later protocol work explores bidirectional
communication and more standardized client support.

00:00:43.713 --> 00:00:49.907
Separate the enduring requirement from a particular
transport choice: the service needs feedback

00:00:49.907 --> 00:00:55.908
about progress, recoverable state and a clear policy
for clients whose networks disappear for

00:00:55.908 --> 00:00:57.005
extended periods.
